systemctl show fixtures for parseShow (internal/providers/nexusd/system.go)

Each file is the output, byte for byte, of

    systemctl show <unit> -p ActiveState -p SubState -p Result -p MainPID -p NRestarts

captured from real systemd in the T1 harness container by TC-SM-129
(test/integration/nexusd_selfmgmt_test.go, TestTCSM129_*), which logs each
capture between "CAPTURE <file> BEGIN" and "CAPTURE <file> END" lines.

systemd version: systemd 259 (259.9-0ubuntu0.1), Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS
(BASE=ubuntu:latest), arm64. Captured by `make test-harness` (WI 7.2) in
the run whose logs are build/harness/logs/20261113T210341Z (2026-11-13).

The unit is nexusd-fidelity.service: a copy of deploy/nexusd.service whose
ExecStart names /usr/local/bin/nexusd-fidelity instead of
/usr/local/bin/nexusd, so nexusd.service itself is not touched. Type,
Restart (on-failure, default RestartSec 100 ms), the default start limit
(StartLimitBurst=5 in StartLimitIntervalSec=10s) and the sandbox are the
shipped unit's.

    active.txt              the real nexusd v9.9.1, after READY=1
                            (Type=notify)
    activating.txt          a binary that never sends READY=1
                            (`exec sleep 600`), before the start timeout
    auto-restart.txt        the v9.9.4 stub (serve exits 1), between its
                            first failed start and the Restart=on-failure
                            re-spawn; caught by a tight poll
    failed-start-limit.txt  the same stub once its re-spawns met the start
                            limit, read right after a further blocking
                            `systemctl restart` was refused

Property order is systemd's own (ActiveState, SubState, MainPID, Result,
NRestarts), not the order of the -p flags; parseShow does not depend on it.

What these captures show about systemd 259 (TC-SM-129 logs the full
journal):

  - At the start limit the unit is failed with Result=exit-code, not
    start-limit-hit: the journal says "Start request repeated too quickly"
    and then "Failed with result 'exit-code'". A blocking restart refused
    by the limit leaves Result=exit-code too.
  - NRestarts is 5 there: 1 explicit start and 4 re-spawns ran, and the
    5th scheduled re-spawn ("restart counter is at 5") was the start the
    limit refused.
  - In auto-restart, NRestarts is still 0: it counts when the re-spawn
    runs, not when it is scheduled.

The previous, hand-written files (WI 4.1) encoded Result=start-limit-hit
with NRestarts 4; the real values replace them.
